CertHub.register({"id":"pcep","vendor":"Python Institute","name":"PCEP – Certified Entry-Level Python Programmer","short":"PCEP","exam":"PCEP-30-02","blurb":"Entry-level Python certification covering syntax, data types, operators, control flow, collections, functions and exceptions, for new programmers.","status":"check","statusNote":"pythoninstitute.org could not be opened from the research environment on Sept 24, 2026, so the official page was not read directly. Exam code PCEP-30-02, the four sections with weights 18/29/25/28, 30 items, 40 minutes and a 70% pass mark come from search-engine excerpts of the official PCEP-30-0x syllabus page and PDF. A third-party site reports a refreshed PCEP version planned for Q3 2026; confirm the current exam code and weights on the official syllabus page before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[],"examInfo":{"questions":"30 items (single-choice, multiple-choice, drag and drop, gap fill, code insertion)","minutes":40,"pass":"70%","extra":"About 5 more minutes are set aside for the NDA and tutorial. Taken online via OpenEDG Testing Service."},"examSim":{"questions":30,"minutes":40},"sources":[{"label":"Python Institute PCEP certification page","url":"https://pythoninstitute.org/pcep"},{"label":"PCEP-30-0x exam syllabus","url":"https://pythoninstitute.org/pcep-exam-syllabus"}],"planWeeks":6,"hoursPerWeek":"4–6","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Computer programming and Python fundamentals","w":18,"topics":["Fundamental terms: interpreting vs compiling, lexis, syntax and semantics, source code and the Python interpreter","Python logic and structure: keywords, instructions, indentation and comments","Literals: Boolean, integer, float, scientific notation and string literals","Binary, octal and hexadecimal integer literals (0b, 0o, 0x)","Variables and naming rules, reserved keywords and PEP 8 naming conventions","Numeric operators: ** * / % // + - and the difference between / and //","String operators (+ and *), assignment and compound assignment operators (+=, *=, etc.)","Operator priority and binding, including right-to-left ** and unary minus","Bitwise operators: ~ & ^ | << >>","Boolean and relational operators, float accuracy and rounding surprises","Type casting with int(), float(), str() and bool()","Console I/O: print() with sep= and end=, input() returning a string, converting input to numbers"],"notes":["Syllabus section 1 (objectives 1.1–1.5)"],"labs":["Install Python 3 from python.org (or use your OS package), open the REPL with `python3` and evaluate 7 // 2, -7 // 2, 7 % 2, 2 ** 3 ** 2 and 0.1 + 0.2; explain each result.","In VS Code with the free Python extension, write a script that asks for two numbers with input(), converts them with int() and float(), and prints their sum using print(..., sep=\" | \", end=\"!\\n\").","Build a small table in the REPL of 0b1010, 0o17, 0xFF, bin(), oct() and hex(), then practise ~, &, | and ^ on 6 and 3 until you can predict every answer before pressing Enter."]},{"id":2,"name":"Control flow: conditional blocks and loops","w":29,"topics":["if, if-else and if-elif-else statements, and why the order of elif conditions matters","Multiple conditions with and, or and not; truthy and falsy values","Nested conditional statements and indentation","The pass instruction as a placeholder body","while loops, loop conditions and avoiding infinite loops","for loops over range() with start, stop and step, including negative steps and empty ranges","Iterating over strings, lists and other sequences with for","break and continue, and how break affects only the innermost loop","while-else and for-else: when the else clause runs and when break skips it","Nested loops and counting iterations","The value of the loop variable after a for loop ends"],"notes":["Syllabus section 2 (objectives 2.1–2.2)"],"labs":["Write a number-guessing game in VS Code that uses a while loop, if-elif-else hints and break when the guess is right; add a for-else version that allows only five tries.","Predict, then run, ten short range() loops in `python3` (range(2, 10, 3), range(5, 0, -2), range(10, 0) and so on) and note every case where your prediction was wrong.","Use the free Python Tutor website (pythontutor.com) to step through a nested loop with break and continue and watch each variable change line by line."]},{"id":3,"name":"Data collections: tuples, dictionaries, lists and strings","w":25,"topics":["Lists: building, indexing (including negative indexes) and slicing","List methods and functions: append(), insert(), index(), remove(), sort(), len(), sorted(), del","Iterating through lists, in and not in, list comprehensions with conditions","Copying vs aliasing lists: b = a compared with a[:] or list(a)","Nested lists and matrices (list of lists)","Tuples: building (including one-item tuples), indexing, slicing, immutability and tuples vs lists","Dictionaries: building, indexing, adding, changing and removing keys","Iterating dictionaries with keys(), values() and items(); checking whether a key exists","Strings: indexing, slicing (including [::-1]), immutability and comparison","Escaping with \\, quotes and apostrophes inside strings, multi-line strings","Common string methods: split(), join(), upper(), lower(), strip(), find(), count(), replace()"],"notes":["Syllabus section 3 (objectives 3.1–3.4)"],"labs":["Write a gradebook script that stores student names and score lists in a dictionary, adds and deletes students, and prints each average with a for loop over items().","In `python3`, show the aliasing trap: set b = a, append to b, print a; then repeat with b = a[:] and explain the difference in a comment.","Build a word-count tool that reads a sentence with input(), cleans it with lower() and strip(), splits it into words and counts them in a dictionary; test it with punctuation and extra spaces."]},{"id":4,"name":"Functions and exceptions","w":28,"topics":["Decomposition: splitting a program into functions","Defining and invoking functions; functions must be defined before they are called","return and yield, returning several values as a tuple, the None value","Recursion, base cases and RecursionError","Parameters vs arguments; positional, keyword and mixed argument passing","Default parameter values and why defaults must follow required parameters","Name scopes, shadowing and the global keyword; UnboundLocalError","The exception hierarchy: BaseException, Exception, SystemExit, KeyboardInterrupt, ArithmeticError, LookupError","Common built-in exceptions: ZeroDivisionError, IndexError, KeyError, TypeError, ValueError, NameError","try-except, except with several exceptions, bare except and except Exception","Ordering except branches from specific to general","Propagating exceptions through function boundaries and deciding where to handle them"],"notes":["Syllabus section 4 (objectives 4.1–4.5)"],"labs":["Rewrite the gradebook from domain 3 as functions (add_student, average, report) with default parameters, and call them with positional and keyword arguments.","Write a safe calculator that uses try-except to handle ValueError from int(input()) and ZeroDivisionError from division, then swap the order of an ArithmeticError branch and see what changes.","Write recursive factorial and countdown functions, remove the base case on purpose to see RecursionError, then write a small generator with yield and loop over it."]}],"study":{"1":[["What is the difference between / and // in Python?","/ is true division and always returns a float (7 / 2 is 3.5). // is floor division: it rounds down, so 7 // 2 is 3 and -7 // 2 is -4. With a float operand // returns a float, such as 7.0 // 2 = 3.0."],["Explain lexis, syntax and semantics.","Lexis is the set of words and symbols a language allows (keywords, operators, literals). Syntax is the rules for combining them into valid statements. Semantics is what a valid statement means; code can be syntactically correct but still wrong in meaning."],["Why does input() + 1 fail, and how do you fix it?","input() always returns a string, and a string cannot be added to an int, so a TypeError is raised. Convert first: int(input()) + 1 or float(input()) + 1."],["What is the order of operator priority you must know for PCEP?","From highest: ** (right-to-left), unary + - ~, * / // %, binary + -, shifts << >>, &, ^, |, comparisons, not, and, or. Use parentheses when in doubt."],["Why is 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 False?","Floats are stored in binary with limited precision, so 0.1 and 0.2 are approximations and their sum is 0.30000000000000004. Compare with a tolerance or round() for money-style values."]],"2":[["When does the else clause of a loop run?","It runs when the loop finishes normally: the while condition becomes false or the for loop runs out of items (including an empty range). It is skipped if the loop ends with break."],["Compare break and continue.","break exits the innermost loop immediately. continue skips the rest of the current pass and goes to the next iteration. Neither affects an outer loop."],["Predict range(10, 0), range(0, 10, 3) and range(5, 0, -1).","range(10, 0) is empty because the default step is +1. range(0, 10, 3) gives 0, 3, 6, 9. range(5, 0, -1) gives 5, 4, 3, 2, 1; the stop value is never included."],["Why does the order of elif branches matter?","Conditions are tested top to bottom and only the first true branch runs. Put narrower tests (score >= 90) before broader ones (score >= 60), or the broad one will catch everything."],["What is pass for?","pass is a statement that does nothing. It fills a block that Python requires syntactically, such as an empty if, loop or function body you have not written yet."]],"3":[["What is the difference between b = a and b = a[:] for lists?","b = a makes both names refer to the same list, so changes through one are seen through the other. a[:] (or list(a)) creates a new list with the same items, so the two can change independently."],["How do you make a one-item tuple, and why?","Write a trailing comma: (5,) or 5,. (5) is just the int 5 in parentheses. The comma, not the parentheses, is what makes a tuple."],["What does the in operator check for lists, strings and dictionaries?","For a list it checks whether an item is present. For a string it checks for a substring. For a dictionary it checks the keys, not the values; use value in d.values() for values."],["Name three ways to remove data from lists and dictionaries.","del lst[i] or del lst[i:j] removes by index; lst.remove(x) removes the first matching value; del d[key] removes a dictionary key (a missing key raises KeyError). d.pop(key) removes and returns the value."],["Why can't you change a character in a string, and what do you do instead?","Strings are immutable, so s[0] = 'H' raises TypeError. Build a new string instead, for example 'H' + s[1:] or s.replace('h', 'H', 1)."]],"4":[["What does a function return if it has no return statement?","It returns None. Printing the result of such a call prints None, which often surprises beginners who call print() inside the function."],["What rules apply when mixing positional and keyword arguments?","Positional arguments come first, then keyword arguments, and each parameter may receive only one value. In the definition, parameters with defaults must come after those without."],["Explain shadowing and the global keyword.","Assigning to a name inside a function creates a local variable that hides (shadows) any global with the same name. To change the global instead, declare global name inside the function. Reading a global without assigning to it needs no declaration."],["Why should specific except branches come before general ones?","Python runs the first branch whose exception type matches, including parent classes. If except ArithmeticError comes first, a later except ZeroDivisionError can never run."],["Which exceptions does except Exception not catch?","Exceptions that inherit directly from BaseException: SystemExit (raised by exit()), KeyboardInterrupt (Ctrl+C) and GeneratorExit. That lets programs still be stopped."],["What happens to an exception that a function does not handle?","It propagates to the caller, then to its caller, and so on. If nothing handles it, the program stops with a traceback. You can choose to handle it at whichever level can respond sensibly."]]},"qCount":97,"hasLessons":true});
